1
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:06,820
This is Trigun.

2
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:33,520
Oh, my God.

3
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:30,830
You

4
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:39,557
Him again?

5
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:41,557
Something has to be done!

6
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:43,440
Gotta get some help.

7
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:06,620
Meryl, I don't see a soul anywhere.

8
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:10,093
Nobody told me this became a ghost town.

9
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:12,657
I didn't hear anybody
say anything about that.

10
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:15,217
I really hope there's a well around it.

11
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:16,637
Wishful thinking doesn't help.

12
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:17,597
Just don't think about it.

13
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:19,856
It only makes the truth more painful.

14
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:39,380
You were right, the truth hurts.

15
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:40,978
I hate being right.

16
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:42,610
Huh?

17
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:44,281
Land subsidence?

18
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:46,130
Tarnation!

19
00:02:46,213 --> 00:02:50,637
First the war dries up, then that dang
criminal, the humanoid typhoon, shows up.

20
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:52,317
This town ain't fit to live in no more.

21
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:53,820
Yeah, bash the stampede.

22
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:54,997
The new hired gun at Saison.

23
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:55,910
Oh, shut.

24
00:02:55,993 --> 00:02:56,823
This town is finished.

25
00:02:56,906 --> 00:02:57,736
Bad times are here.

26
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:05,560
Hmm. Merrill. Uh-huh.

27
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:07,270
All right.

28
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:08,660
I'm so excited.

29
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:10,917
Now we can get water from that mansion.

30
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:12,180
Oh, I give up.

31
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:14,760
Excuse me.

32
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:16,800
Is this the Cesar residence?

33
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:19,497
My name is Merrill Stryfe,
and I'm here on behalf?

34
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:21,557
of the Bernardelli Insurance Society.

35
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:23,497
I'd like to talk to you
for a minute, please.

36
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:26,717
Yeah, and I'm her partner, Millie
Thompson, and we're very thirsty.

37
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:28,340
Hey, don't say that.

38
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:32,257
I just need to ask you a few
questions about Vash the Stampede.

39
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:34,281
I heard that he was staying here.

40
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:44,160
Thank you very much.

41
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:46,340
Yes, we certainly appreciate this.

42
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:50,230
Hello?

43
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:52,490
Uh-oh.

44
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:54,557
Hey, what are you doing here?

45
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:55,757
You're not supposed to be here.

46
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:57,420
I work here now. I'm Mr.

47
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:00,932
Cliff's hired bodyguard, and
I'm his exclusive ace gunman.

48
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:03,457
Hey, is that for me? How nice.

49
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:04,920
Oh, you shouldn't have.

50
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:08,160
You're really a bodyguard?

51
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:09,840
These are good.

52
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:11,178
I can't believe it.

53
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:14,180
We're happy to see you
again, Mr. Ace Gunman.

54
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:17,057
It's an amazing coincidence.

55
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:18,357
These are really delicious.

56
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:19,600
Would you like one?

57
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:22,360
Well, yes, I'd love one. Huh?

58
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:25,097
You have got to keep your
mind on business, Millie.

59
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:26,010
Huh?

60
00:04:26,093 --> 00:04:27,417
All right, Ace, I want to know.

61
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:29,794
What's your real reason for being here?

62
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:31,710
Hmm...

63
00:04:31,793 --> 00:04:33,379
Well, now that was an odd response.

64
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:36,400
Excuse me, I don't mean to interrupt you,

65
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:39,541
but are these young ladies
friends of yours, Vash?

66
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:40,880
What? Vash?

67
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:41,877
He's not Vash!

68
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:43,097
What the hell are you talking about?

69
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:45,877
In any event, you two seem
to be here for a reason.

70
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:46,790
Huh?

71
00:04:46,873 --> 00:04:48,817
We shouldn't be discussing this out here.

72
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:50,037
Won't you please come in?

73
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:52,820
Uh, yeah, but I don't think that we...

74
00:04:57,400 --> 00:04:59,140
My name is Cliff Cesar.

75
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:02,440
I make my living selling
water in this town,

76
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,297
but recently somebody's
been trying to kill me.

77
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:07,857
That could be bad for
business and my health.

78
00:05:07,940 --> 00:05:10,280
So I advertise for a good bodyguard,

79
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:13,480
for someone tough and smart
like Vash the Stampede.

80
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,440
I originally put his name down as a joke.

81
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:19,337
It never occurred to me
that it would be taken

82
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:22,117
seriously and that the real
thing would actually show up.

83
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:23,397
Isn't that right, Vash?

84
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:24,310
Yeah.

85
00:05:24,393 --> 00:05:25,360
Yeah, it's me, all right.

86
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:28,128
Now that I'm here, you
have nothing to fear.

87
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:30,457
The man who will slaughter the innocent!

88
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:32,817
Does the most evil of
evil deeds with a price

89
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:35,117
of 60 billion double dollars on his head?

90
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:36,420
Yes, I am that man!

91
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:39,840
What a nice, dependable
guy, and modest as well.

92
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:42,137
Isn't it wonderful?

93
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:45,573
With Bash around, the people
of the mansion will be safe.

94
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:48,937
This may sound rude, but I think
you should have your eyes checked.

95
00:05:49,020 --> 00:05:52,237
Now listen, young lady, it's very
important that I have a good bodyguard.

96
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:55,085
Let me explain to you
just how important it is.

97
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:01,780
Her name is Marianne Alekhuizen.

98
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:05,217
She's the granddaughter of the
head of the Stantal Federal Bureau,

99
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:07,947
and she's staying at
the mansion as my guest.

100
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:12,157
The truth is, we had an attempted
break-in here five days ago,

101
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:14,240
and frankly, I'm a little worried.

102
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,697
I wouldn't have hired a bodyguard if
I thought they were coming after me,

103
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:26,309
but Marianne's safety
is my responsibility,

104
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:28,857
and I do not want
anything happening to her.

105
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:32,999
Well, now you know the whole story,
and that is why I need your help.

106
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:34,897
You can definitely count on me, Mr.

107
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:35,810
Cliff.

108
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:38,417
I don't let anyone in my sights get away,

109
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:40,997
and I'm known for my bullets
never missing their mark,

110
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:43,962
especially if it's the
heart of a beautiful lady.

111
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:47,370
Hey

112
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:50,197
I'm happy to have you protect me mr.

113
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:51,110
Vash.

114
00:06:51,193 --> 00:06:52,023
I really am.

115
00:06:52,106 --> 00:06:54,713
Oh, yeah A lot of people say
that I hear it all the time.

116
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:55,960
Is that right?

117
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:57,180
I believe it

118
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:06,500
I Need to talk to you?

119
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:10,660
What's the matter my dear
listen to me mr. Cliff.

120
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:12,660
I think you're a nice man

121
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,937
How can you believe that weird guy
out there is Vash the Stampede,

122
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:18,255
the legendary gunman?

123
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:21,120
That droopy-eyed, cocky,
upside-down-haired,

124
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:23,197
women-chasing lunatic
who's nuts for donuts

125
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:25,097
and gorges them like
a starved hog in heat.

126
00:07:25,180 --> 00:07:26,827
You honestly think it's him?

127
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:28,420
She's really very nice.

128
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:29,657
She bought the donuts.

129
00:07:29,740 --> 00:07:31,617
She's really very nice and loud.

130
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:35,876
I am telling you for the last time,
you are being deceived by that man!

131
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:40,000
On the contrary, I'm
doing the deceiving. Huh?

132
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:42,457
I think you got a good look at the city.

133
00:07:42,540 --> 00:07:44,037
With that kind of mess out there,

134
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:46,037
all it took was a simple
little rumor that Vash

135
00:07:46,120 --> 00:07:48,657
the Stampede was working
for me as a bodyguard.

136
00:07:48,740 --> 00:07:50,937
The town folk panicked
and headed for the hills,

137
00:07:51,020 --> 00:07:53,491
and I'm expecting my enemy to do the same.

138
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:54,730
Hello?

139
00:07:54,813 --> 00:07:56,417
This ad of yours caught my eye.

140
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:57,794
Well, congratulations.

141
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:01,100
As of today, you are now
Vash the Stampede. Huh?

142
00:08:01,540 --> 00:08:04,237
Vash the Stampede, it's
a pleasure to meet you.

143
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:05,397
It could have been anybody.

144
00:08:05,480 --> 00:08:06,580
He just showed up.

145
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:07,760
I see.

146
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:09,277
He's a huge inconvenience,

147
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:11,897
but using him in your plan this
way makes a lot more sense.

148
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:15,745
Actually, this whole thing's gone
a lot farther than I expected.

149
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:18,757
The scoundrel who's been
after me is a tall, blond man,

150
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:22,487
packing a silver weapon and a
reputation as an ace gunfighter.

151
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:24,270
Huh?

152
00:08:24,353 --> 00:08:25,317
Did you hear that, Millie?

153
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:27,140
Yes, I did. Every word.

154
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:29,440
This is an absolute godsend.

155
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:31,965
What humorous people they are.

156
00:08:32,060 --> 00:08:35,057
Yeah, it's usually best just
to leave airheads alone.

157
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:36,240
Hey, you know, Mr.

158
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:37,190
Cliff,

159
00:08:37,273 --> 00:08:39,717
if you don't mind, My partner Millie and
I would like to be your bodyguards too,

160
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:42,137
For extra protection
only naturally we won't

161
00:08:42,220 --> 00:08:44,037
demand to feel like that
greedy faker over there,

162
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:46,473
so there'd be no risk to you whatsoever?

163
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:49,637
Well, that'd be fine with me.

164
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:50,550
Thank you, sir.

165
00:08:50,633 --> 00:08:51,479
You won't be sorry

166
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:55,857
This is not what I expected
to do as a bodyguard

167
00:08:55,940 --> 00:08:58,217
every journey begins with a single step.

168
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:00,006
We just have to have patience

169
00:09:00,820 --> 00:09:01,980
Is it ready yet?

170
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:07,220
We'll take it out when
it is wow scary later

171
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:10,717
I'd like to show that guy something scary.

172
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:12,477
Nothing has gone right since we met him.

173
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:14,417
I don't think that's true at all.

174
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:16,147
I'm having a wonderful time.

175
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:19,499
Plus, we get the hell to
all the water we can drink.

176
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:22,640
Boy, has she got a short fuse.

177
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:28,115
I can hear the sound of running water.

178
00:09:28,300 --> 00:09:31,888
And I believe it's coming from
the shower in Mary Ann's room.

179
00:09:34,380 --> 00:09:35,321
Lifeline secure?

180
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:40,540
When favorable, the plan is perfect.

181
00:09:41,140 --> 00:09:42,580
I've got the guts.

182
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:48,140
I'm coming, Marianne!

183
00:09:55,900 --> 00:09:58,220
Miss Marianne, the supper is...

184
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:01,040
Where are you?

185
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:02,780
Oh.

186
00:10:04,760 --> 00:10:05,660
Hello.

187
00:10:10,580 --> 00:10:13,417
What in the world are you doing
hanging outside the window?

188
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:16,577
I'm, uh, well, diligently
patrolling the roof area for spooks.

189
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:18,500
Yeah, that's it. Oh, wow.

190
00:10:18,760 --> 00:10:19,980
That sounds scary.

191
00:10:20,260 --> 00:10:22,378
You bodyguards sure are busy people.

192
00:10:22,660 --> 00:10:24,757
Oh, yeah, I almost forgot to tell you.

193
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:25,722
Supper's ready.

194
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:27,070
Thanks a lot.

195
00:10:40,460 --> 00:10:42,720
This is Mr. Cliff's study.

196
00:10:44,160 --> 00:10:44,990
Marianne.

197
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:49,160
Ah, this place sure is fancy.

198
00:10:49,720 --> 00:10:51,900
Is water really that profitable, Mr.

199
00:10:52,020 --> 00:10:52,850
Cliff?

200
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:55,697
There's been a drought
for the past few years.

201
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:58,517
I'm very lucky to have been
able to enjoy a few luxuries.

202
00:10:58,600 --> 00:11:02,557
Because my father, who was a plant engineer,
purchased land with a water supply.

203
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:04,357
It's a rare commodity around here.

204
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:07,137
I'd imagine that would tend to
make a lot of people jealous.

205
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:10,279
Yep, I'm afraid a lot of
folks want my water rights.

206
00:11:10,940 --> 00:11:11,770
Apparently,

207
00:11:11,853 --> 00:11:13,797
they think I'm monopolizing
it for selfish reasons

208
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:16,417
and don't want to share
the wealth with them.

209
00:11:16,500 --> 00:11:18,880
But the water supply is not limitless.

210
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:23,837
Unless it's managed properly by someone
who knows how, it will completely dry up.

211
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:28,449
But it doesn't change the fact that
you're profiting from a natural resource.

212
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:31,478
I'm sorry, it's none of my business.

213
00:11:31,740 --> 00:11:33,916
No, you're absolutely right, my dear.

214
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,297
But I can't let the water be stolen by

215
00:11:36,380 --> 00:11:39,086
reckless people who don't
care if it dries up.

216
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:43,917
They may call me a coward, but I intend
to do what it takes to protect the water!

217
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:45,620
That's most admirable, Mr.

218
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:46,570
Cliff

219
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:49,000
Please excuse me, Mr.

220
00:11:49,140 --> 00:11:53,258
Cliff, I'm not feeling very well I
think I'm going to go to bed early.

221
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,277
She may look calm, but she's
probably feeling quite ill at ease.?

222
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:04,020
Perhaps it was not such a
good idea to invite her here.

223
00:12:04,220 --> 00:12:05,460
Uh, I suppose.

224
00:12:05,900 --> 00:12:09,277
Well, I think I'll make the rounds
and make sure this place is secure.

225
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:12,654
I should at least pretend to
look like a real bodyguard.

226
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:17,997
As an extra safety measure, I've asked
Marianne to keep her curtain drawn. You...

227
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:18,910
I...

228
00:12:18,993 --> 00:12:21,107
I would never dream of
doing anything like...

229
00:12:22,540 --> 00:12:23,920
I mean, good thinking.

230
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:25,617
Very sensible idea, Mr.

231
00:12:25,700 --> 00:12:26,530
Cliff.

232
00:12:26,613 --> 00:12:27,557
Ha ha ha ha ha!

233
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:28,820
Ha ha ha ha ha!

234
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:30,310
Later!

235
00:12:46,120 --> 00:12:50,057
I'm really exhausted, Meryl, from
all the cooking we had to do today.

236
00:12:50,140 --> 00:12:51,557
This is only the beginning.

237
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:53,377
There's no telling when
the culprit will show up.

238
00:12:53,460 --> 00:12:54,697
We have to be alert in our duties.

239
00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:56,737
There'll be no sleeping for us tonight?

240
00:12:56,820 --> 00:13:03,437
May I come in?

241
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:05,377
One moment, please!

242
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:10,080
Come in.

243
00:13:10,920 --> 00:13:14,397
Excuse me, something wrong?

244
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:15,817
Uh, no.

245
00:13:15,900 --> 00:13:18,577
Were you looking at the stars?

246
00:13:18,660 --> 00:13:22,420
Yes, I was thinking.

247
00:13:24,580 --> 00:13:27,992
Even though most of my memories
there are unpleasant ones.

248
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:31,660
Thoughts of home, whether good or bad,

249
00:13:31,860 --> 00:13:34,801
are positive indicators
of a journey of the heart.

250
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:39,742
It's always been my dream to travel
by myself to any place I want.

251
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:42,958
But I could never persuade
my father to let me do it.

252
00:13:43,140 --> 00:13:47,258
So I've only been allowed to stay
at the homes of his closest friends.

253
00:13:48,160 --> 00:13:50,737
This is hardly what I'd call a
journey of the heart, you know?

254
00:13:50,820 --> 00:13:51,650
Oh?

255
00:13:57,860 --> 00:14:01,684
Someday, you'll be able to take
a journey to anywhere you desire.

256
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:04,449
Although it may not
exactly be by yourself.

257
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:08,304
I'm not sure I understand
